# DEBT-0011 — Evolution Engine Stage-17 Memory Update Bypasses Kernel Gate & Reference Monitor

**Status**: OPEN  
**Filed**: 2026-07-30  
**Priority**: High  
**Category**: Architectural Deviation / Gate Bypass  

---

## 1. Compromise
In [runtime/evolution.py](file:///Users/bob/GitHub/paeos/runtime/evolution.py), Stage 17 (`_memory_update`) mutates institutional memory (`ScarStore`) directly by invoking `propose_scar(draft)`. It does not mint capability tokens, invoke `Gate.propose_transition()`, or record a `scar_committed` event to the kernel ledger.

## 2. Reason
The Evolution Layer was built in B2.F as a standalone runtime module operating post-run over a `RunOutcome`. To allow scars to accumulate across backlog runs in `SelfHostRunner`, `EvolutionLayer` was wired directly to `ScarStore` without setting up a full kernel transition gate for each scar injection.

## 3. Ideal Solution
Route Stage 17 institutional memory mutations through a formal kernel gate transition (`StageId.MEMORY_UPDATE`) authorized by capability tokens and committed to the single-writer ledger as a `scar_committed` event.

## 4. Repayment Conditions
Repay during Phase 3 Evolution Layer hardening prior to multi-agent self-hosting.

## 5. Estimated Impact
High. Allowing runtime code to mutate institutional memory out-of-band bypasses TCB Reference Monitor governance (PAEOS-7 §4.4, PAEOS-7 §7), violating the core requirement that all state transitions must be gate-verified.
